  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 343 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nCurrently, isolated to scattered showers were indicated over far\nsouthern North Dakota. Isolated showers were also located in the\nnorth. Skies were cloudy south and partly to mostly cloudy \nnorth. Low stratus has developed over southwest ND where rain \nhad exited and skies had temporarily cleared. As of 2 AM CDT \nthere were no visibility restrictions at observation sites, but \nwill continue to monitor. \n\nFor today, showers will track east across far southern ND, and\nmay push as far north as the I-94 corridor, but any\naccumulations greater than a tenth of an inch will remain along\nthe South Dakota border. Isolated showers are possible in the\nnorth as well.\n\nThis afternoon and this evening, there will be low chances for \nthunderstorms from west central into north central North Dakota.\nAn instability axis remains situated from eastern Montana into \nsoutheast Saskatchewan/southern Manitoba. The elevated \ninstability, combined with the surface convergence along the \ninverted trough extending into Canada, ahead of an approaching \ncold front, may produce enough forcing to spark a few \nthunderstorms. The threat for severe storms looks to be minimal \nbut the RAP/HRRR both indicate a narrow area of 1000-1500 J/kg \nof CAPE from west central ND into northwest ND this afternoon \ninto early evening. There is some capping there, but during the\nmax heating of the day, it becomes pretty minimal over this \narea. Bulk shear is generally in the 25-30 knot range. Will \nmonitor but currently there is only a general thunderstorm risk\nfrom SPC and this seems reasonable. As the Canadian cold front \nsinks south into the far northern CWA, showers may linger from \nthrough the evening across the far north.\n\nSaturday, high pressure builds behind the Canadian cold front as\nit pushes south through the forecast area. This will tamper out\nthe elevated instability that is over the north today. The \nfocus shifts to western ND Saturday through Sunday. An upper \nlevel low moves through the Northern Rockies Saturday through \nSaturday night and is situated over the Northern High Plains by \nSunday morning. Saturday will be wet across much of central and \neastern Montana. The question is how far east into North Dakota \nwill any precipitation extend. At this time it looks like most\nactivity will remain along and west of the Highway 85 corridor \nduring the day Saturday, with a better chance of precipitation \nmaking it as far east as the Highway 83 corridor later Saturday \nnight into Sunday. We're talking low to medium chances (20 to \n40%) for rain making it to the Highway 83 corridor. Medium to \nhigh chances (40 to 90%) for rain remain over western ND during\nthis timeframe, with the highest of these chances over the \nsouthwest portion of the state. Through the 2 day period of \nSaturday through Sunday, the NBM depicts a 50 to 80 percent \nchance of qpf amounts of at least a half inch in the far west \ncentral and southwest North Dakota. This tapers quickly to less \nthan a 20 percent chance from the Highway 85 corridor to the \nHighway 83 corridor. \n\nThe upper level system remains to our south on Monday with upper\nlevel ridging building over the area through mid to late week.\nCurrently the NBM is depicting mainly low pops south and east\nTuesday night through Wednesday night as there is low confidence\nin a weak wave moving through the area during this timeframe. \n\nTemperatures wise, as was mentioned yesterday, there is a \nsignal for a gradual warming trend through the week, but with \nrising temperatures through the workweek comes increasing \nensemble spread. Currently the NBM is depicting some upper 70s \nand lower 80s for western ND by late in the work week, which is \na little above normal (normal highs are in the lower 70s).",
